# Using a Pre-Build Script

Complementary to the post-build hook script, you can also create a pre-build hook script that will be run before the invocation of Maven. This can be used to do some preparations for the build.

The default name of the pre-build script is `prebuild`, which resolves to `prebuild.groovy`, or to the deprecated `prebuild.bsh`. Write hook scripts in Groovy; see [BeanShell scripts](${esc.hash}BeanShell_scripts) for the deprecation of BeanShell.

In a pre-build script you can prepare test resources for your project.

You can also define user properties, which the plugin passes to the build of the test project as `-D` arguments.

Here is an example of a Groovy script (`prebuild.groovy`) that finds a free port and passes it to the build:

```groovy
def userProperties = context.get('userProperties')

def port = new ServerSocket(0).withCloseable { it.localPort }
userProperties.put('serverHost', 'localhost')
userProperties.put('serverPort', String.valueOf(port))
```

Now you can use it in test project:

```xml
<project>
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.example</groupId>
        <artifactId>my-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>@project.version@</version>
        <configuration>
          <server>${serverHost}:${serverPort}</server>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
        ....
       </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
```

# Using a Post-Build Script

Here is an example of how the Invoker Plugin can be used to run a set of Maven projects and then verify their output with a [Groovy](https://groovy-lang.org/) script. The name of the script file in this case is `verify`; the default name is `postbuild`.

```xml
<project>
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <artifactId>maven-invoker-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <debug>true</debug>
          <projectsDirectory>src/it</projectsDirectory>
          <preBuildHookScript>setup</preBuildHookScript>
          <postBuildHookScript>verify</postBuildHookScript>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>integration-test</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>run</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
       </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
```

Below is an example post-build Groovy script (`verify.groovy`) that checks for the existence of a JAR file after the build has run. If the JAR file does not exist, the assertion fails, which causes the Invoker Plugin to log that the build failed. More precisely, any non-null return value which does not equal `true` will be interpreted as a failure condition. And of course, if the script exits abnormally due to an exception, the plugin will flag the corresponding build as a failure, too.

Example `verify.groovy`

```groovy
def file = new File( basedir, "target/my-test-project-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar" )
assert file.isFile()
```

# BeanShell scripts

Since plugin version 3.11.0, BeanShell (`.bsh`) scripts are deprecated, and support for them will be removed in a
future release. This covers every script the plugin runs: the selector script and the pre-build and post-build hook
scripts, including the indexed ones such as `postbuild.1.bsh`. Write new scripts in Groovy and port existing ones.
Removing BeanShell across Maven is tracked in [apache/maven#13207](https://github.com/apache/maven/issues/13207).

The plugin still runs BeanShell scripts. For each `.bsh` script it runs, it logs a warning once per plugin execution,
both in the Maven output and in the `build.log` of the test project:

```text
[WARNING] BeanShell scripts are deprecated, port /path/to/project/target/it/first-it/verify.bsh to Groovy: https://maven.apache.org/plugins/maven-invoker-plugin/examples/pre-post-build-script.html
```

To port a script, rename it to `.groovy` and adjust the code. Most BeanShell code is valid Groovy; the `verify.bsh`
below behaves like the `verify.groovy` above:

```java
import java.io.*;

File file = new File( basedir, "target/my-test-project-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar" );
if ( !file.isFile() )
{
    throw new FileNotFoundException( "Could not find generated JAR: " + file );
}
```

Delete the `.bsh` file once the `.groovy` file exists: when you configure a script name without an extension and both
files exist, the plugin runs the `.bsh` one.

# Pre and Post Build Script for multiple project executions

You can run multiple project executions in a single test by defining several `invoker.goals.N` in the `invoker.properties` file.

In this case, the pre and post build hook scripts can be executed for each invocation by adding the index to the script name. For example, if you have two executions, you can have the following scripts:

- `prebuild.groovy` - preprocess before all executions
- `prebuild.1.groovy` - preprocess before the first execution
- `postbuild.1.groovy` - verify the first execution
- `prebuild.2.groovy` - preprocess before the second execution
- `postbuild.2.groovy` - verify the second execution
- `postbuild.groovy` - verify all executions

Missing script files will be ignored.

# Script bytecode level

The pre-/post-build Groovy scripts are compiled to the bytecode level of the project under test, so a project that
sets `maven.compiler.release` or `maven.compiler.target` needs no further configuration. The level is taken from the
first of these that is set:

1. the [`scriptTargetBytecode`](../run-mojo.html#scriptTargetBytecode) parameter,
2. the `maven.compiler.release` property,
3. the `maven.compiler.target` property.

The properties are looked up on the command line (`-D`) first and then in the project. When none of them is set, the
scripts are compiled to the bytecode level of the JDK running Maven. To keep that level in a project that does set
one of the properties, set the parameter to it explicitly:

```xml
<scriptTargetBytecode>${esc.d}{java.specification.version}</scriptTargetBytecode>
```

# Predefined global variables

To allow the scripts to access some useful data about the test project, the following global variables will be defined by the Invoker Plugin before running the script:

|Name|Type|Description|Since|
|:---|:---|:---|:---|
|`basedir`|`java.io.File`|The absolute path to the base directory of the test project.|1.0|
|`localRepositoryPath`|`java.io.File`|The absolute path to the local repository used for the Maven invocation on the test project.|1.3|
|`context`|`java.util.Map`|The storage of key-value pairs used to pass data from the pre-build hook script to the post-build hook script.|1.4|
|`context.get('userProperties')`|`java.util.Properties`|The user properties for executing project|3.7.0|
|`context.get('buildLog')`|`java.io.File`|The build log of the current job. It is `null` with `noLog`, and in a selector script it holds no build output yet because no build has run. It is a per-job entry of `context` rather than a global variable because jobs can run in parallel.|3.11.0|
|`mavenVersion`|`java.lang.String`|The version of Maven executing on the test project.|1.9|

Additional variables that can be accessed in the hook scripts can be defined through the [`scriptVariables`](../run-mojo.html#scriptVariables) parameter in the Invoker Plugin's configuration.
